#!/usr/bin/env python3
"""OnionHost update engine (Gate 4 / DESIGN-v3 A6).

Runs from onionhost-update.timer (OnCalendar 00:00 UTC +
RandomizedDelaySec=24h, so the fleet never moves in lockstep and a
mirror cannot count installs by the hour). Everything is fetched by
apt through container U's client-only Tor (golden config:
52onionhost-tor-updates + the shipped tor+http method). Nothing is
ever fetched on the clearnet, and nothing via gateway A.

Trust / anti-rollback rules (A6, DQ4, Y8):
  * apt verifies the repo signature (project key) and Debian's; this
    tool never passes --allow-downgrades or any insecure flag.
  * A per-package HIGH-WATER MARK is persisted on the host
    (/var/lib/onionhost/update/highwater.json). A candidate below the
    mark - e.g. a mirror replaying an old, validly signed index - is
    REFUSED and reported, never installed.
  * Each signed onionhost package carries a security FLOOR
    (/usr/share/onionhost/floor.json inside the .deb). The highest
    floor ever seen is persisted and never decreases.
  * After an install, the stack is restarted and health-checked
    (onionhost-probe: the site must answer through the real onion
    path). If the check fails, the previous version is restored -
    but ONLY if it is at/above the floor. If it is below the floor,
    the machine FAILS CLOSED (Y8): the gateway is stopped, the site
    goes off, and the state is recorded for the Dashboard and
    Maintenance. Serving on a below-floor stack to stay up is the
    one outcome this tool exists to prevent.
  * The health check's time budget is sized against MEASURED
    republication latency, not optimism (rig battery part 4,
    2026-10-14): across 10 stack restarts on real hardware the
    median time back to a 200 was 91 s, but one restart took
    324 s - beyond the probe's own ~3-4 minute envelope, so a
    healthy update landing in that tail would have been rolled
    back, and could have failed closed, for the sole reason
    that Tor was slow to republish. health_ok() therefore runs
    the probe up to HEALTH_ATTEMPTS times with a pause between
    attempts (nominal budget ~6-9 minutes) before judging a
    stack unhealthy. Only the budget changed: an update that
    still does not answer after it rolls back / fails closed
    exactly as before.
  * A SETTLE drawn from HEALTH_SETTLE_MIN..MAX (default
    30 min to 6 h) decouples what an observer of the box's Tor
    presence could time against the package fetch (Repo
    Review 5 D-1; placement corrected by the deanonymization
    audit, R-13): on the install path the settle now sits
    BEFORE the stack restart, so the republication itself -
    not just the local probe - lands at a drawn time unrelated
    to the fetch. The rollback and fail-closed-recovery
    restarts cannot wait out a settle (a judged-bad stack must
    not keep serving; a down site's return is the recovery
    duty), so their judgements keep the settle between the
    restart and the first probe. The probe itself is executed
    argv-form, never through a shell (Repo Review 5 F-P1-1).
  * A later good update (at/above the floor) recovers the machine
    from fail-closed automatically on the next run.
  * The fail-closed state is persisted in a dedicated marker file
    (fail-closed.marker, next to highwater.json/floors.json) and
    the recovery duty keys on it - NOT on status.json, which is
    the Dashboard's record of the last run's outcome. Only a
    healthy outcome clears the marker; a run that merely failed
    to fetch never overwrites a fail-closed record (Gate 4
    follow-up, found by the A23 SMF recipe slice).

Recipes are NOT update channels (A26, owner decision 2026-10-21):
a recipe package (onionhost-recipe-*) installs an app ONCE; after
that the app is the owner's and this tool never installs, upgrades,
rolls back, or judges a recipe package again. An installed app
whose recipe package has a newer version in the repository is a
normal, healthy state - it is reported in status.json
(owner_maintained) for the Dashboard, never as an error and never
a rollback or fail-closed trigger. Recipe security floors gate
install time only (the applier refuses a below-floor first
install); no floor can strand or fail-close an app that is
already installed. "Installed" is the applier's state record
(/var/lib/onionhost/recipes/<id>/state.json carrying a completed
apply), NOT the dpkg state of the recipe package - factory
images dpkg-install every recipe package on every box, selected
or not. Recipe packages are still HELD across the Debian phase
below, so the generic upgrade cannot fire a recipe postinst
behind this tool's back (and the A26 postinst gate refuses to
re-apply over an installed app even if one is upgraded by hand).

Debian base packages ride the same run (same apt invocation config,
same timing): apt-get upgrade after the managed packages, best
effort - a Debian failure is logged but never blocks or rolls back
the project package, and apt's own no-downgrade rules apply.

State written for the Dashboard (status text only, no user action):
/var/lib/onionhost/update/status.json - see wizard.py's mapping.

Exit codes: 0 ok / nothing to do / recovered; 2 refused downgrade
seen (state safe); 3 fetch failed (updates paused, site untouched);
4 update failed but rolled back to a healthy legal version;
5 FAIL-CLOSED.
"""
import json
import os
import random
import shlex
import subprocess
import sys
import time
from datetime import datetime, timezone

STATE_DIR = os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_UPDATE_STATE",
                           "/var/lib/onionhost/update")
ARCHIVES = os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_APT_ARCHIVES",
                          "/var/cache/apt/archives")
# The pending-reboot flag package scripts leave behind; the
# Dashboard's restart card keys on the record this tool makes
# of it (audit H2/M3, task T3).
REBOOT_REQUIRED_PATH = os.environ.get(
    "ONIONHOST_REBOOT_REQUIRED", "/run/reboot-required")
# The managed set: the platform package ONLY (A26). The install /
# rollback / fail-closed machinery below judges this set and
# nothing else. Recipe packages are discovered separately and are
# observe-only - see _recipe_packages() and the header's A26 rules.
PLATFORM_PACKAGE = "onionhost"
MANAGED = [PLATFORM_PACKAGE]


def _recipe_packages():
    """dpkg-installed recipe packages (onionhost-recipe-*, A23).

    Discovery only: under A26 these packages never take part in
    the managed phase. A dpkg-installed recipe package is NOT an
    installed app (the factory image registers every recipe
    package on every box); whether an app is installed is the
    applier's state record - see _installed_recipe()."""
    out = []
    try:
        listing = subprocess.run(
            ["dpkg-query", "-W", "-f", "${Package}\n",
             "onionhost-recipe-*"],
            capture_output=True, text=True)
        for name in sorted(listing.stdout.split()):
            if name not in out:
                out.append(name)
    except OSError:
        pass
    return out


RECIPE_PKGS = _recipe_packages()
# Everything this tool knows by name: the unhold/hold hygiene
# covers recipe packages too, so a hold leaked by a killed run
# (or by a pre-A26 updater, which held them as managed packages)
# cannot wedge anything, and the Debian phase never upgrades
# them behind the managed phase's back.
ALL_KNOWN = MANAGED + RECIPE_PKGS
# The applier's state root and the payload root (manifest names),
# with the same env override names onionhost-recipe uses.
RECIPE_STATE_ROOT = os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_RECIPE_STATE",
                                   "/var/lib/onionhost/recipes")
RECIPE_PAYLOAD_ROOT = os.environ.get(
    "ONIONHOST_RECIPE_PAYLOAD_ROOT", "/usr/share/onionhost/recipes")
HEALTH_CMD = os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_HEALTH_CMD",
                            "/usr/local/bin/onionhost-probe")
STACKCTL = os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_STACKCTL", "")
# Per-invocation cap for one probe run: the probe's own retry
# envelope (10 attempts, 20 s socket timeouts, 20 s sleeps)
# needs at most ~380 s; 600 leaves generous headroom.
HEALTH_TIMEOUT = 600
# Health-check budget (see the header): how many probe runs a
# single health_ok() judgement may take, and the pause between
# them. Two runs + the pause put the judgement at ~6-9 minutes
# nominal - past the 324 s republication tail the rig battery
# measured - without weakening the fail-closed rule that ends
# the budget. Both are env-tunable for the state-machine tests.
HEALTH_ATTEMPTS = int(os.environ.get("ONIONHOST_HEALTH_ATTEMPTS",
                                     "2"))
HEALTH_RETRY_DELAY = int(os.environ.get(
    "ONIONHOST_HEALTH_RETRY_DELAY", "45"))
# Settle (Repo Review 5 D-1; placement corrected by the
# deanonymization audit, R-13): a wait drawn fresh per use from
# [SETTLE_MIN, SETTLE_MAX]. It decouples the Tor-visible events
# of an update run from the package fetch that began it:
# without it, the republication that follows an install, and
# the self-probe's rendezvous to this box's own onion, follow
# the mirror fetch within minutes, and an observer who could
# see both could correlate them. On the install path the
# settle sits between the install and the restart, so the
# republication itself is what lands at the scattered time;
# rollback and recovery judgements keep it between their
# restart and the probe, because those restarts cannot wait
# (see health_ok_after_restart). The verdict the probe drives
# is unchanged - a failed probe still rolls back or fails
# closed exactly as before; it is simply reached at a scattered
# time. Env-tunable for the state-machine tests.
HEALTH_SETTLE_MIN = int(os.environ.get(
    "ONIONHOST_HEALTH_SETTLE_MIN", "1800"))
HEALTH_SETTLE_MAX = int(os.environ.get(
    "ONIONHOST_HEALTH_SETTLE_MAX", "21600"))

LOG_PATH = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "update.log")


def now():
    return datetime.now(timezone.utc).strftime("%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ")


LOG_MAX_BYTES = 1 << 20         # 1 MiB writer-side cap (R-24)
LOG_KEEP_BYTES = 1 << 18        # newest 256 KiB survive a rotation


def _cap_log(path):
    """Bound the update log (deanonymization audit R-24): left
    alone it grows one timestamped line-set per run, forever -
    a seized running box would hand over a complete dated
    history of every update, recovery and failure. When the
    file passes the cap it is rewritten to its newest slice
    (from a line boundary) behind a rotation note, so the
    recent operational record survives and the long tail does
    not. Best effort, exactly like the append it bounds."""
    try:
        if os.path.getsize(path) <= LOG_MAX_BYTES:
            return
        with open(path, "rb") as f:
            f.seek(-LOG_KEEP_BYTES, os.SEEK_END)
            tail = f.read()
        nl = tail.find(b"\n")
        if nl >= 0:
            tail = tail[nl + 1:]
        note = ("%s onionhost-update: log rotated - older "
                "entries discarded by the size cap\n"
                % now()).encode()
        with open(path, "wb") as f:
            f.write(note + tail)
    except OSError:
        pass


def log(msg):
    line = "%s onionhost-update: %s" % (now(), msg)
    print(line, flush=True)
    try:
        with open(LOG_PATH, "a") as f:
            f.write(line + "\n")
    except OSError:
        pass
    _cap_log(LOG_PATH)


# Golden files are never hand-edited on a healthy machine; force
# dpkg's deterministic conffile handling so an unattended upgrade
# can never stall on an interactive conffile prompt.
DPKG_OPTS = ["-o", "Dpkg::Options::=--force-confdef",
             "-o", "Dpkg::Options::=--force-confold"]

PROJECT_SOURCES = os.environ.get(
    "ONIONHOST_PROJECT_SOURCES",
    "/etc/apt/sources.list.d/onionhost.sources")


def apt_update_project():
    """Refresh ONLY the project repository's lists.

    The two update channels must fail independently (DESIGN-v3 A6:
    Debian updates flow regardless of the project mirror, and vice
    versa): a Debian mirror outage must not freeze the signed
    project package - the channel that carries security floors -
    and a project-repo outage must not block Debian's. So the
    managed packages are judged on a project-only apt update;
    the full Debian update runs afterwards, best-effort.
    Returns True on success."""
    parts = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "apt-sourceparts")
    os.makedirs(parts, exist_ok=True)
    link = os.path.join(parts, "onionhost.sources")
    try:
        if os.path.islink(link) or os.path.exists(link):
            os.unlink(link)
        os.symlink(PROJECT_SOURCES, link)
    except OSError as e:
        log("cannot stage project source: %s" % e)
        return False
    log("apt-get update - project repository only (via container U)")
    rc = run(["apt-get",
              "-o", "Dir::Etc::sourceparts=%s" % parts,
              "-o", "Dir::Etc::sourcelist=/dev/null",
              "-o", "APT::Get::List-Cleanup=0",
              "update"], timeout=1200)
    return rc == 0


def run(cmd, timeout=1800):
    log("+ %s" % " ".join(cmd))
    proc = subprocess.run(cmd, stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                          stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True,
                          timeout=timeout)
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        log("  %s" % line)
    return proc.returncode


def dpkg_compare(a, op, b):
    return subprocess.run(["dpkg", "--compare-versions", a, op, b]
                          ).returncode == 0


def installed_version(pkg):
    proc = subprocess.run(["dpkg-query", "-W", "-f=${Version}",
                           pkg], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                          stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True)
    v = proc.stdout.strip()
    return v if proc.returncode == 0 and v else None


def repo_best_version(pkg):
    """The best version the REPOSITORIES offer for pkg, or None.

    This is NOT apt's candidate: apt never selects a downgrade, so
    its candidate hides exactly the case the high-water mark exists
    to catch - a replayed repository offering only old versions
    shows candidate == installed and looks "current". apt-cache
    madison lists every version per source; ignore the dpkg status
    pseudo-source and take the max of what repos actually serve."""
    proc = subprocess.run(["apt-cache", "madison", pkg],
                          stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
    best = None
    for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
        parts = [p.strip() for p in line.split("|")]
        if len(parts) != 3 or parts[2].endswith("status"):
            continue
        v = parts[1]
        if best is None or dpkg_compare(v, "gt", best):
            best = v
    return best


def load_json(path, default):
    try:
        with open(path) as f:
            return json.load(f)
    except (OSError, ValueError):
        return default


def save_json(path, data):
    tmp = path + ".tmp"
    with open(tmp, "w") as f:
        json.dump(data, f, indent=1, sort_keys=True)
        f.write("\n")
        f.flush()
        os.fsync(f.fileno())
    os.rename(tmp, path)


def deb_floor(deb_path, pkg):
    """Read the security floor for pkg from inside a .deb (the floor
    file is part of the signed package contents)."""
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(
            ["dpkg-deb", "--fsys-tarfile", deb_path],
            stdout=subprocess.PIPE, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
        import io
        import tarfile
        tf = tarfile.open(fileobj=io.BytesIO(proc.stdout), mode="r:*")
        # The platform floor lives at usr/share/onionhost/
        # floor.json; a recipe's at usr/share/onionhost/recipes/
        # <id>/floor.json. Any floor.json member counts; the one
        # naming pkg wins.
        for name in tf.getnames():
            if name.endswith("/floor.json"):
                try:
                    data = json.load(tf.extractfile(name))
                except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - keep looking
                    continue
                if pkg in data:
                    return data[pkg]
        return None
    except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - absent floor = no constraint
        log("floor: could not read floor from %s: %s" % (deb_path, e))
        return None


def find_deb(pkg, version):
    for name in sorted(os.listdir(ARCHIVES)):
        if name.startswith(pkg + "_") and name.endswith(".deb"):
            if (deb_version(os.path.join(ARCHIVES, name)) == version):
                return os.path.join(ARCHIVES, name)
    return None


def deb_version(path):
    proc = subprocess.run(["dpkg-deb", "-f", path, "Version"],
                          stdout=subprocess.PIPE, text=True)
    return proc.stdout.strip() if proc.returncode == 0 else None


def stackctl(*args):
    if STACKCTL:
        return run([STACKCTL] + list(args))
    units = ["onionhost-a.service", "onionhost-b.service",
             "onionhost-app.service"]
    action = args[0] if args else "restart"
    rc = 0
    for unit in units:
        r = subprocess.run(["systemctl", action, unit],
                           stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL,
                           stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL).returncode
        if action == "stop" and r != 0:
            rc = r
    return rc


def stop_gateway():
    """Fail-closed action (Y8): the site goes OFF - gateway first,
    then the site containers."""
    if STACKCTL:
        run([STACKCTL, "stop-gateway"])
        run([STACKCTL, "stop"])
        return
    subprocess.run(["systemctl", "stop", "onionhost-a.service"],
                   stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)
    subprocess.run(["systemctl", "stop", "onionhost-b.service",
                    "onionhost-app.service"],
                   stdout=subprocess.DEVNULL, stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL)


def _probe_once():
    """One probe run against the stack: True iff the site
    answered 200 through the onion path within the probe's own
    retry envelope."""
    # Argv-form exec (Repo Review 5 F-P1-1): HEALTH_CMD is split
    # to a fixed argv and run WITHOUT a shell - a root updater
    # should not route its health check through /bin/sh. The env
    # override still names the command (tests stub it), but it
    # can no longer smuggle shell syntax.
    argv = shlex.split(HEALTH_CMD)
    if not argv:
        log("health: no probe command configured")
        return False
    try:
        proc = subprocess.run(argv,
                              stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                              stderr=subprocess.STDOUT, text=True,
                              timeout=HEALTH_TIMEOUT)
        lines = proc.stdout.splitlines()
        for line in lines[-5:]:
            log("  health: %s" % line)
        if proc.returncode != 0:
            return False
        # onionhost-probe prints PROBE_OK for ANY HTTP response
        # (slice 4); a health check needs the 200 specifically.
        for line in lines:
            if line.startswith("PROBE_OK"):
                return " 200" in line
        return True
    except subprocess.TimeoutExpired:
        log("health: timed out after %ds" % HEALTH_TIMEOUT)
        return False
    except OSError as e:
        log("health: could not run %r: %s" % (argv[0], e))
        return False


def health_ok():
    """Judge the (just restarted) stack healthy or not.

    One probe run is NOT the whole budget: a restarted onion
    service republishes on Tor's schedule, and the rig battery
    measured a 324 s tail against a ~3-4 minute single-run
    envelope. So a failed run is retried after a pause, up to
    HEALTH_ATTEMPTS runs, before the stack is judged unhealthy
    and the rollback / fail-closed machinery engages. The
    judgement at the end of the budget is exactly as strict as
    it always was."""
    for attempt in range(1, HEALTH_ATTEMPTS + 1):
        if _probe_once():
            return True
        if attempt < HEALTH_ATTEMPTS:
            log("health: no healthy answer yet (attempt %d of "
                "%d) - waiting %ds and checking again before "
                "judging; a restarted onion service can take "
                "minutes to republish"
                % (attempt, HEALTH_ATTEMPTS, HEALTH_RETRY_DELAY))
            time.sleep(HEALTH_RETRY_DELAY)
    return False


def _settle_seconds():
    """Draw a settle from the D-1 window (per call)."""
    lo = HEALTH_SETTLE_MIN
    hi = max(HEALTH_SETTLE_MAX, lo)
    if hi <= 0:
        return 0
    return random.SystemRandom().uniform(lo, hi)


def _sleep_settle(context):
    """Sleep one drawn settle, logged under the caller's label.

    The settle is the D-1 decoupling: something an observer of
    this box's Tor presence could time against the package fetch
    is made to happen at a drawn, scattered time instead of
    within minutes of the fetch that started the run."""
    settle = _settle_seconds()
    if settle > 0:
        log("health: waiting %ds (%s - decoupled from this "
            "update run)" % (int(settle), context))
        time.sleep(settle)
    return settle


def health_ok_after_restart():
    """health_ok() with the D-1 settle in front of it.

    Used by the restart paths where the restart itself cannot
    wait out a settle: the rollback (a stack judged unhealthy
    must not keep SERVING while a 30-minute-to-6-hour delay
    runs - Y8) and fail-closed recovery (the site is off; its
    return is the duty, and in the common recovery no package
    fetch precedes that restart in the same run beyond the
    index refresh). The INSTALL path is different: there the
    restart is what republishes the onion, so the settle sits
    BEFORE it and the judgement is plain health_ok() - see
    main() (deanonymization audit R-13). The judgement itself -
    and every rollback / fail-closed consequence hanging off
    it - is health_ok() unchanged."""
    _sleep_settle("post-restart settle, drawn per judgement")
    return health_ok()


# A26 owner-maintained report: one entry per INSTALLED app whose
# recipe package has a newer version in the repository. Populated
# by main()'s observation pass and carried in every status write
# after it: a normal, healthy state the Dashboard words as
# information, never as an error.
OWNER_MAINTAINED = []

# Set by main() once this run's repository check has succeeded;
# write_status maintains last_successful_check from it (audit
# M3): a run that could not reach the repository must not make
# an old result look freshly checked.
CHECK_SUCCEEDED = False


def _reboot_state():
    """(required, reason) for the pending-reboot record.

    Sources: /run/reboot-required - the flag package scripts
    write, whose sibling .pkgs file (and on some releases the
    file itself) names what asked for the reboot - and
    needrestart's batch report when the image carries it (a
    running kernel older than the newest installed one means a
    reboot is pending whether or not a package script flagged
    it). The shipped image carries no needrestart and Debian
    kernel packages do not write the flag file, so when neither
    source fires, the running release is compared against the
    installed kernels directly (one /lib/modules directory per
    installed kernel). Nothing here may raise: the record is
    written on every status path, including failure paths."""
    required = False
    reasons = []
    pkgs = []
    for path in (REBOOT_REQUIRED_PATH,
                 REBOOT_REQUIRED_PATH + ".pkgs"):
        try:
            with open(path) as f:
                pkgs += [ln.strip() for ln in f if ln.strip()]
            required = True
        except OSError:
            continue
    if pkgs:
        reasons.append("requested by "
                       + ", ".join(sorted(set(pkgs))[:8]))
    try:
        import shutil
        exe = shutil.which("needrestart")
        if exe:
            proc = subprocess.run(
                [exe, "-b"], stdout=subprocess.PIPE,
                stderr=subprocess.DEVNULL, text=True, timeout=30)
            fields = {}
            for line in proc.stdout.splitlines():
                if ":" in line:
                    k, v = line.split(":", 1)
                    fields[k.strip()] = v.strip()
            cur = fields.get("NEEDRESTART-KCUR")
            exp = fields.get("NEEDRESTART-KEXP")
            if cur and exp and cur != exp:
                required = True
                reasons.append("a newer kernel is installed "
                               "than the one running")
    except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - best-effort record
        pass
    if not required:
        try:
            modules_dir = os.environ.get(
                "ONIONHOST_MODULES_DIR", "/lib/modules")
            running = os.environ.get(
                "ONIONHOST_RUNNING_RELEASE", os.uname().release)

            def _vkey(rel):
                head = rel.split("+")[0].split("-")[0]
                return tuple(int(p) for p in head.split(".")
                             if p.isdigit())

            installed = [d for d in os.listdir(modules_dir)
                         if d[:1].isdigit()]
            if installed and _vkey(max(installed, key=_vkey)) \
                    > _vkey(running):
                required = True
                reasons.append("a newer kernel is installed "
                               "than the one running")
        except Exception:  # noqa: BLE001 - best-effort record
            pass
    return required, "; ".join(reasons)


def write_status(state, detail, packages):
    status_path = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "status.json")
    prev = load_json(status_path, {})
    reboot_required, reboot_reason = _reboot_state()
    if reboot_required:
        # The pending restart's age is measured from when this
        # updater first recorded it, carried across runs.
        since = prev.get("reboot_required_since") \
            if prev.get("reboot_required") else None
        if not since:
            since = now()
    else:
        since = None
    rec = {}
    rec["state"] = state
    rec["detail"] = detail
    rec["checked_at"] = now()
    rec["packages"] = packages
    rec["owner_maintained"] = OWNER_MAINTAINED
    rec["reboot_required"] = reboot_required
    rec["reboot_reason"] = reboot_reason
    rec["reboot_required_since"] = since
    rec["last_successful_check"] = (
        now() if CHECK_SUCCEEDED
        else prev.get("last_successful_check"))
    save_json(status_path, rec)


def _installed_recipe(recipe_id):
    """The completed-install state record for recipe_id, or None.

    A26's "installed": the applier (onionhost-recipe) writes its
    state record only when an apply COMPLETES (after the tree
    swap / binary placement, and again when the install hook
    finishes), always with installed=True and the applied
    version. A first install that failed before completion
    leaves no such record, so the install path stays open for
    it; a rollback rewrites the same record with the restored
    version - the app is still installed."""
    state = load_json(os.path.join(RECIPE_STATE_ROOT, recipe_id,
                                   "state.json"), {})
    if state.get("installed") and state.get("version"):
        return state
    return None


def _recipe_app_name(recipe_id):
    """The app's display name from its payload manifest (the
    Dashboard's wording), falling back to the recipe id."""
    manifest = load_json(os.path.join(RECIPE_PAYLOAD_ROOT,
                                      recipe_id, "manifest.json"),
                         {})
    return manifest.get("name") or recipe_id


MARKER_PATH = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "fail-closed.marker")


def marker_present():
    return os.path.exists(MARKER_PATH)


def write_marker(packages, floors):
    """Record the fail-closed fact in its own state file.

    Written whenever the machine fails closed (and once when a
    legacy fail-closed status is adopted - see main); carries the
    installed version and the standing floor per MANAGED package,
    the facts the recovery duty judges by. Recipe packages never
    appear here (A26): nothing about an owner-maintained app may
    feed the fail-closed record."""
    save_json(MARKER_PATH,
              {"since": now(),
               "packages": {pkg: {"installed": installed_version(pkg),
                                  "floor": floors.get(pkg)}
                            for pkg in packages
                            if pkg in MANAGED}})


def clear_marker():
    try:
        os.unlink(MARKER_PATH)
    except OSError:
        pass


def disk_floor_for(pkg):
    """The floor currently standing on disk for pkg: the platform
    floor file, or any installed recipe's floor file."""
    import glob
    candidates = ["/usr/share/onionhost/floor.json"]
    candidates += sorted(glob.glob(
        "/usr/share/onionhost/recipes/*/floor.json"))
    for path in candidates:
        try:
            with open(path) as f:
                data = json.load(f)
            if pkg in data:
                return data[pkg]
        except (OSError, ValueError):
            continue
    return None


def main():
    global CHECK_SUCCEEDED
    os.makedirs(STATE_DIR, exist_ok=True)
    os.makedirs(os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "prev"), exist_ok=True)
    hw_path = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "highwater.json")
    fl_path = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "floors.json")
    highwater = load_json(hw_path, {})
    floors = load_json(fl_path, {})
    packages = {}
    worst = 0

    # Recovery duty: if the previous run failed CLOSED, the stack
    # was deliberately stopped. A later run must not mistake
    # "installed == candidate" for "all is well" and leave the site
    # off forever - when the installed version is at/above the
    # floor, this run restarts the stack and re-checks health even
    # if there is nothing new to install.
    prev_status = load_json(os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "status.json"), {})
    # The duty keys on the marker; status.json is only the legacy
    # carrier (a machine closed by an older updater has the status
    # but no marker - it is adopted just below).
    recovering = marker_present() or \
        prev_status.get("state") == "fail-closed"

    # Baseline: a factory install has no state files yet. The mark
    # starts at whatever is installed (the ISO is the floor of
    # history), the floor at the installed package's own floor file.
    for pkg in MANAGED:
        inst = installed_version(pkg)
        if inst and pkg not in highwater:
            highwater[pkg] = inst
        disk_floor = disk_floor_for(pkg)
        if disk_floor and pkg not in floors:
            floors[pkg] = disk_floor
        packages[pkg] = {"installed": inst}

    # A26 observation baseline: recipe packages are recorded for
    # the Dashboard record only. No high-water mark is seeded and
    # no floor is read for them: nothing about a recipe package
    # may judge, refuse, or fail-close anything ever again.
    for pkg in RECIPE_PKGS:
        packages[pkg] = {"installed": installed_version(pkg),
                         "recipe": pkg[len("onionhost-recipe-"):],
                         "owner_maintained": True}

    if recovering and not marker_present():
        log("adopting a legacy fail-closed status into the marker")
        write_marker(packages, floors)

    # Managed packages move only through this phase; clear any
    # hold a previous run's Debian phase may have leaked (it
    # holds the set across its generic upgrade - see there).
    # Recipe packages are unheld too: a pre-A26 updater held them
    # as managed packages, and a leaked hold must not wedge a
    # package the owner handles by hand.
    for pkg in ALL_KNOWN:
        run(["apt-mark", "unhold", pkg])

    if not apt_update_project():
        if recovering:
            # The site is OFF for safety; a fetch failure changes
            # nothing about that and must not overwrite the
            # fail-closed record with an "unaffected" one.
            log("fetch failed while failed closed - updates pause; "
                "the fail-closed state stands")
            return 3
        write_status("fetch-failed",
                     "Update check could not reach the repository. "
                     "Your site is unaffected.", packages)
        log("fetch failed - updates pause, site untouched")
        return 3

    # The repository check succeeded: status writes from here
    # may advance last_successful_check (audit M3).
    CHECK_SUCCEEDED = True

    # A26: observe recipe packages - never touch them. For each
    # recipe whose app is INSTALLED on this box (the applier's
    # state record, not dpkg), a newer package in the repository
    # is reported as owner-maintained: a normal, healthy state
    # the Dashboard shows as information, never an error and
    # never a trigger for rollback or fail-closed. Under A26
    # this pass is observation only - it installs nothing,
    # returns nothing, and the run's verdict is not its to move.
    for pkg in RECIPE_PKGS:
        recipe_id = pkg[len("onionhost-recipe-"):]
        inst = installed_version(pkg)
        cand = repo_best_version(pkg)
        packages[pkg].update({"candidate": cand})
        state = _installed_recipe(recipe_id)
        if not state:
            log("%s: payload present, app not installed - "
                "nothing to report" % pkg)
            continue
        packages[pkg].update({"app_installed": state.get("version")})
        if cand and inst and dpkg_compare(cand, "gt", inst):
            OWNER_MAINTAINED.append({
                "package": pkg, "recipe": recipe_id,
                "app": _recipe_app_name(recipe_id),
                "installed": inst, "available": cand})
            log("%s: a newer packaged version (%s) is available; "
                "the installed app is owner-maintained (A26) and "
                "is NOT updated by the platform - the expected "
                "state, not an error" % (pkg, cand))
        else:
            log("%s: app installed (package %s) - "
                "owner-maintained (A26)" % (pkg, inst))

    for pkg in MANAGED:
        inst = installed_version(pkg)
        # Decisions are driven by the best version the repositories
        # offer, NOT apt's candidate (which can never be a downgrade
        # and so can never trip the high-water refusal - see
        # repo_best_version).
        cand = repo_best_version(pkg)
        packages[pkg] = {"installed": inst, "candidate": cand,
                         "highwater": highwater.get(pkg),
                         "floor": floors.get(pkg)}
        if cand is None:
            log("%s: no candidate in repository" % pkg)
            continue
        mark = highwater.get(pkg)
        if mark and dpkg_compare(cand, "lt", mark):
            log("REFUSED %s: repository's best version %s is below "
                "the high-water mark %s - a replayed or rolled-back "
                "repository cannot downgrade this machine"
                % (pkg, cand, mark))
            write_status("refused-downgrade",
                         "An out-of-date update source was refused.",
                         packages)
            worst = max(worst, 2)
            continue
        if inst is not None and cand == inst:
            if recovering:
                floor = floors.get(pkg)
                if floor is not None and dpkg_compare(inst, "lt",
                                                      floor):
                    log("%s: installed %s is below the floor %s - "
                        "cannot recover on it; staying failed closed"
                        % (pkg, inst, floor))
                    return 5
                log("%s: recovering from fail-closed - restarting "
                    "the stack on the installed %s and re-checking "
                    "health" % (pkg, inst))
                stackctl("restart")
                if health_ok_after_restart():
                    log("%s: recovery healthy - site is back" % pkg)
                    recovering = False
                    # The installed version is now health-verified:
                    # it becomes the high-water mark, exactly as if
                    # this run had installed it.
                    if dpkg_compare(inst, "gt",
                                    highwater.get(pkg, "0")):
                        highwater[pkg] = inst
                        save_json(hw_path, highwater)
                        packages[pkg].update({"highwater": inst})
                    write_status("up-to-date",
                                 "Everything is current.", packages)
                    clear_marker()
                    continue
                log("%s: recovery health check failed - staying "
                    "failed closed" % pkg)
                stop_gateway()
                stackctl("stop")
                write_status(
                    "fail-closed",
                    "An update could not be applied safely, so your "
                    "site was turned off rather than left running "
                    "out-of-date software. It will come back "
                    "automatically when a fixed update is available.",
                    packages)
                write_marker(packages, floors)
                return 5
            log("%s: %s is current" % (pkg, inst))
            continue

        # Learn the candidate's floor BEFORE installing: the floor
        # travels inside the signed package, and Y8 judges the OLD
        # version against the NEW package's floor.
        if run(["apt-get", "install", "-y", "--download-only"] + DPKG_OPTS + [pkg],
               timeout=1800) != 0:
            if recovering:
                log("%s: download failed while failed closed - "
                    "the fail-closed state stands" % pkg)
                return 3
            write_status("fetch-failed",
                         "Update download failed. Your site is "
                         "unaffected.", packages)
            return 3
        cand_deb = find_deb(pkg, cand)
        cand_floor = deb_floor(cand_deb, pkg) if cand_deb else None
        if cand_floor:
            if pkg not in floors or dpkg_compare(
                    cand_floor, "gt", floors[pkg]):
                floors[pkg] = cand_floor
                save_json(fl_path, floors)
            log("%s: floor from signed package: %s (floor on record: "
                "%s)" % (pkg, cand_floor, floors[pkg]))

        prev_version = inst
        prev_deb = os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "prev", pkg + ".deb")
        prev_saved = os.path.exists(prev_deb) and \
            deb_version(prev_deb) == prev_version
        if not prev_saved and prev_version:
            # A version the updater did not itself install - the
            # factory version from the ISO, say - has no rollback
            # copy yet, so a failed FIRST update could only fail
            # closed. Fetch that version's .deb from the (signed,
            # already-fetched) repository now, before anything
            # changes, and keep it as the rollback copy. If the
            # repo no longer carries it, carry on without: the
            # fail-closed fallback below remains the safe floor.
            try:
                subprocess.run(
                    ["apt-get", "download",
                     "%s=%s" % (pkg, prev_version)],
                    cwd=ARCHIVES, capture_output=True, text=True,
                    timeout=900)
                old_deb = find_deb(pkg, prev_version)
                if old_deb:
                    subprocess.run(["cp", "-f", old_deb, prev_deb])
                    prev_saved = deb_version(prev_deb) == \
                        prev_version
                    if prev_saved:
                        log("%s: saved a rollback copy of the "
                            "installed %s" % (pkg, prev_version))
            except Exception as e:  # noqa: BLE001 - best effort
                log("%s: could not fetch a rollback copy of %s: %s"
                    % (pkg, prev_version, e))
        log("installing %s %s -> %s" % (pkg, inst, cand))
        rc = run(["apt-get", "install", "-y"] + DPKG_OPTS + [pkg], timeout=1800)
        if rc == 0:
            # R-13: the settle belongs BEFORE this restart. The
            # restart is what republishes the onion - if it
            # follows the package fetch within seconds, the new
            # descriptors land on the network in the fetch's own
            # time slot, and a settle placed between restart and
            # probe only delays the local judgement, decoupling
            # nothing an observer can see. Sleeping first, with
            # the new version already installed, makes the
            # republication itself land at a drawn time. The
            # judgement then runs plain health_ok(): its probe
            # budget is sized for republication tails, and a
            # second settle would only double the delay.
            _sleep_settle("pre-restart settle, drawn per install")
            stackctl("restart")
            healthy = health_ok()
        else:
            # Install failed: restart (the stack may be
            # half-configured) and judge unhealthy without a
            # probe - exactly the pre-R-13 behaviour, which the
            # short-circuited expression did anyway.
            stackctl("restart")
            healthy = False
        if healthy:
            highwater[pkg] = cand
            save_json(hw_path, highwater)
            if cand_deb:
                subprocess.run(["cp", "-f", cand_deb, prev_deb])
                save_json(os.path.join(STATE_DIR, "prev",
                                       pkg + ".version"),
                          {"version": cand})
            packages[pkg].update({"installed": cand,
                                  "highwater": cand,
                                  "floor": floors.get(pkg)})
            log("%s: updated to %s and healthy" % (pkg, cand))
            recovering = False  # a healthy install IS the recovery
            write_status("updated", "Updated to %s." % cand, packages)
            clear_marker()
            continue

        log("%s: update to %s FAILED its health check" % (pkg, cand))
        floor = floors.get(pkg)
        can_rollback = (prev_version and prev_saved and
                        (floor is None or
                         dpkg_compare(prev_version, "ge", floor)))
        if can_rollback:
            log("rolling back to %s (at/above floor %s)"
                % (prev_version, floor))
            run(["dpkg", "-i", prev_deb], timeout=600)
            stackctl("restart")
            if health_ok_after_restart():
                packages[pkg].update({"installed": prev_version,
                                      "floor": floor})
                recovering = False  # healthy rollback is a recovery
                write_status(
                    "update-failed",
                    "An update did not work and was undone. Your "
                    "site is running the previous version.", packages)
                clear_marker()
                worst = max(worst, 4)
                continue
            log("rollback to %s is ALSO unhealthy" % prev_version)
        else:
            log("rollback target %s is below the security floor %s "
                "or unavailable - failing closed" %
                (prev_version, floor))
        # Y8 fail-closed: never keep serving on a stack that is
        # broken or below the floor. The site goes OFF - gateway
        # first (the onion goes dark), then the stack itself.
        stop_gateway()
        stackctl("stop")
        packages[pkg].update({"floor": floor})
        write_status(
            "fail-closed",
            "An update could not be applied safely, so your site "
            "was turned off rather than left running out-of-date "
            "software. It will come back automatically when a fixed "
            "update is available.", packages)
        write_marker(packages, floors)
        return 5

    # Debian base packages: same Tor path, same run, best effort -
    # a Debian mirror failure is logged and changes nothing about
    # the project package's outcome above. The known set is
    # HELD across the generic upgrade: apt-get upgrade sees every
    # configured source (the project repo included) and would
    # otherwise happily re-install a version the managed phase
    # has just rolled back for failing its health check -
    # undoing the rollback before the run even ends (found by
    # the WordPress recipe acceptance, 2026-10-14: a sabotaged
    # recipe was rolled back cleanly and then re-upgraded by
    # this phase in the same run). Recipe packages are held for
    # the A26 reason: upgrading one fires its postinst, and no
    # update run may reach an installed app's tree by any route
    # (the postinst gate is the second lock on the same door).
    # Holds are released at the end of the phase, and the
    # managed phase unholds defensively on its way in (above),
    # so a hold leaked by a killed run cannot wedge the next
    # managed install.
    log("apt-get update + upgrade (Debian base, same Tor path)")
    held = [pkg for pkg in ALL_KNOWN
            if run(["apt-mark", "hold", pkg]) == 0]
    if run(["apt-get", "update"], timeout=1800) != 0:
        log("Debian apt-get update failed for at least one source "
            "- logged only; upgrading from whatever lists did "
            "arrive (e.g. security), the rest retries next run")
    # Audit H2 (T1): the upgrade is split in two. The download
    # runs first, alone, under the timeout - killing a download
    # is safe (apt resumes or re-fetches; nothing is unpacked
    # yet). The install then runs with NO timeout: a kill
    # mid-install is what wedges dpkg (M2), so this call must
    # not be able to receive one. --with-new-pkgs lets both
    # calls take new package names: Debian ships kernel fixes
    # as new ABI packages behind linux-image-amd64, and plain
    # apt-get upgrade holds those back forever. The flag never
    # removes anything.
    dl = run(["apt-get", "--download-only", "-y",
              "--with-new-pkgs"] + DPKG_OPTS + ["upgrade"],
             timeout=3600)
    if dl != 0:
        log("Debian download phase returned %d - the install "
            "phase re-fetches what it needs and is judged by "
            "its own result" % dl)
    rc = run(["apt-get", "-y", "--with-new-pkgs"] + DPKG_OPTS
             + ["upgrade"], timeout=None)
    for pkg in held:
        run(["apt-mark", "unhold", pkg])
    if rc != 0:
        log("Debian upgrade returned %d - logged, project state "
            "unaffected" % rc)

    if recovering:
        # Never reached a healthy state this run (e.g. the repo
        # offered nothing installable): the site stays off and the
        # status must keep saying so.
        write_status(
            "fail-closed",
            "An update could not be applied safely, so your site "
            "was turned off rather than left running out-of-date "
            "software. It will come back automatically when a fixed "
            "update is available.", packages)
        write_marker(packages, floors)
        log("done (exit 5) - still failed closed")
        return 5
    if worst == 0:
        detail = "Everything is current."
        if OWNER_MAINTAINED:
            detail += (" Your %s is yours to update: a newer "
                       "packaged version is available and was "
                       "not applied."
                       % OWNER_MAINTAINED[0]["app"])
        write_status("up-to-date", detail, packages)
    log("done (exit %d)" % worst)
    return worst


if __name__ == "__main__":
    sys.exit(main())
